.TH PKIN 1 "2026-08-30" "SPS 1.0" "SPS Commands"
.SH NAME
pkin \- install or upgrade one SPS binary package
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkin
.RB [ --explicit | --dependency ]
.I package
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkin
is the low-level SPS binary installer. It validates one SPS tar archive,
checks payload hashes, architecture, target paths and ownership conflicts,
installs below
.BR SPS_ROOT ,
and commits plain-text package state. It does not search repositories or solve
dependencies.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --explicit
Add the package to the explicit world set. This is the default.
.TP
.B --dependency
Install without adding the package to the explicit world set.
.SH CONFIGURATION PRESERVATION
Regular files below prefixes configured by
.B SPS_PRESERVE
(default
.BR etc )
are protected when locally modified. On upgrade the local file remains. If the
new packaged default changed, it is written beside the file as
.B .sps-new
(or a versioned .sps-new name when necessary).
.SH HOOKS
Visible package hooks may run before/after install or upgrade. A failing
pre-install hook aborts before payload installation. A failing post-install
hook returns status 10 after committed package state and history are retained.
After a successful post-install,
.B pkin
enables a mapped service for the init recorded in
.I $SPS_ROOT/etc/sps/init
(systemd, OpenRC, s6, runit, dinit, or Shepherd). Stock scripts under
.I $SPS_LIBDIR/setup/sv
are copied when the package did not ship a file for that init.
.SH OWNERSHIP
When run as uid 0, new payload files and package-created directories are
normalized to uid/gid 0 while keeping their packaged modes. Existing shared
directories keep their current ownership and mode.
.SH DATABASE
Installed records live below
.BR $SPS_DB/installed .
.B owners
maps non-directory payload paths to packages and
.B world
contains explicit roots. SPS serializes writers with
.BR $SPS_DB/.lock
and can recover a stale lock only when its recorded numeric PID is verifiably
not alive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, 2 for usage, 3 for unavailable archive, 4 for invalid or
wrong-architecture package, 6 for payload checksum failure, 7 for conflict or
unsafe target path, 8 for permission/filesystem failure, 10 for hook failure,
and 11 for package database failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkpkg (1),
.BR pkdel (1),
.BR pkstat (1),
.BR pkmark (1)
